WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.946 --> 00:00:09.372
As a child, Jane Goodall dreamed of going to Africa to live with wild animals.

2
00:00:10.253 --> 00:00:15.357
Her love of animals was plain to see, but few people could have imagined where it would take

3
00:00:15.397 --> 00:00:20.002
her. During her childhood, Goodall brought many kinds of animals home.

4
00:00:20.862 --> 00:00:25.687
When she was 18 months old, she brought some earthworms into the house and wanted to keep them

5
00:00:25.747 --> 00:00:27.028
as pets in her bed.

6
00:00:27.932 --> 00:00:32.376
She released them only when her mother pointed out that they need to be in the earth to

7
00:00:32.436 --> 00:00:36.280
live. Her curiosity about the natural world was limitless.

8
00:00:37.280 --> 00:00:41.944
At age four, while the family was staying at a farm, she went missing for an afternoon.

9
00:00:42.885 --> 00:00:49.451
Worried, her mother called the police, but Goodall eventually turned up, explaining excitedly that she had hidden in

10
00:00:49.491 --> 00:00:51.613
the hen house to see an egg being laid.

11
00:00:52.654 --> 00:01:00.149
Goodall's curiosity shaped her destiny: she became a world-famous primatologist whose discoveries have changed the way humans see

12
00:01:00.169 --> 00:01:01.130
the natural world.

13
00:01:02.030 --> 00:01:07.872
Goodall was born in London, England, on April 3, 1934, and grew up in the town of Bournemouth

14
00:01:08.072 --> 00:01:09.352
on the southern coast of England.

15
00:01:10.232 --> 00:01:15.074
Her father, Mortimer, was a race-car driver, and her mother, Vanne, was a writer.

16
00:01:15.974 --> 00:01:22.156
Goodall enjoyed backyard expeditions such as capturing insects or digging up worms, and then running to show her

17
00:01:22.196 --> 00:01:23.336
mother each discovery.

18
00:01:24.244 --> 00:01:30.129
Her parents encouraged her, and Mortimer started her lifelong love of primates when he gave Goodall a toy

19
00:01:30.189 --> 00:01:32.191
chimpanzee for her second birthday.

20
00:01:32.871 --> 00:01:36.014
By the age of eight, Goodall had vowed to go to Africa.

21
00:01:36.915 --> 00:01:43.340
Her mother supported her, saying, "Jane, if you really want something, and if you work hard, take advantage

22
00:01:43.380 --> 00:01:47.624
of the opportunities and never give up, you will somehow find a way."

23
00:01:48.608 --> 00:01:54.932
Goodall's passion for animals deepened when she met Rusty, a black mixed-breed dog who belonged to the owner

24
00:01:54.972 --> 00:01:55.932
of a local hotel.

25
00:01:56.733 --> 00:02:03.376
Goodall played with Rusty and taught him tricks, writing in her 1999 book Reason for Hope that Rusty

26
00:02:03.396 --> 00:02:10.801
"made it abundantly clear that animals had personalities, could reason and solve problems, had minds, had emotions."

27
00:02:11.761 --> 00:02:16.784
After completing high school, Goodall had no money for college and little hope of going to Africa.

28
00:02:17.648 --> 00:02:23.610
After her mother told her that secretaries could get jobs anywhere in the world, Goodall entered secretarial school

29
00:02:23.670 --> 00:02:25.691
and found work soon after graduating.

30
00:02:26.531 --> 00:02:32.433
Then, in 1955, she received an invitation to visit a school friend in Kenya, Africa.

31
00:02:33.433 --> 00:02:39.095
In March 1957, after working for two years as a secretary and then waitress to pay for the

32
00:02:39.135 --> 00:02:40.916
trip, Goodall sailed to Africa.

33
00:02:41.800 --> 00:02:47.425
In Kenya, her friend told her she should meet Louis Leakey, a famous anthropologist who was researching human

34
00:02:47.465 --> 00:02:54.972
evolution. By examining the fossils of distant human relatives, Leakey was trying to prove that humans and primates

35
00:02:55.052 --> 00:02:56.433
share common ancestors.

36
00:02:57.314 --> 00:03:02.258
When Goodall visited Leakey at the museum he ran, he asked her questions about the animals featured in

37
00:03:02.278 --> 00:03:02.859
the exhibits.

38
00:03:03.779 --> 00:03:08.744
Although she had no college education, the self-taught Goodall answered his questions well.

39
00:03:09.320 --> 00:03:12.182
Leakey, impressed, hired her as his secretary.

40
00:03:13.242 --> 00:03:18.746
A few months later, Leakey asked Goodall, now 24 years old, to study a group of chimpanzees living

41
00:03:18.786 --> 00:03:19.506
in Tanzania.

42
00:03:20.407 --> 00:03:25.890
She happily agreed, but soon learned that the African government would not permit a young woman to travel

43
00:03:25.950 --> 00:03:26.890
alone in the wilderness.

44
00:03:27.831 --> 00:03:34.135
Goodall's mother came to her rescue, writing to friends, "I shall never know why I suddenly heard myself

45
00:03:34.195 --> 00:03:36.336
say how much I should like to go to Gombe."

46
00:03:37.148 --> 00:03:42.810
In July 1960, the two women set up camp at the Gombe Stream Game Reserve in Tanzania.

47
00:03:43.730 --> 00:03:49.811
From Gombe, Goodall wrote to her family, "It is the Africa of my childhood's dreams, and I have

48
00:03:49.851 --> 00:03:53.312
the chance of finding out things which no one has ever known before."

49
00:03:54.213 --> 00:03:59.294
Although the chimpanzees would not allow her near them for the first few months, Goodall was patient.

50
00:04:00.154 --> 00:04:02.515
They soon grew accustomed to her, and she to them.

51
00:04:03.595 --> 00:04:07.468
As she began to recognize individual chimps, she gave them names.

52
00:04:08.429 --> 00:04:10.969
She called one male chimp David Greybeard.

53
00:04:11.750 --> 00:04:14.851
Soon, Goodall began to make discoveries about the chimpanzees.

54
00:04:15.791 --> 00:04:22.493
She found that, like humans, with whom they share 98 percent of their genes, chimpanzees communicate and have

55
00:04:22.553 --> 00:04:23.593
family relationships.

56
00:04:24.593 --> 00:04:29.735
She saw them hunting for meat, which contradicted the common belief that they were mostly vegetarian.

57
00:04:30.595 --> 00:04:36.534
Her most famous discovery came that fall, when Goodall saw David Greybeard using a stalk of grass to

58
00:04:36.594 --> 00:04:39.456
fish termites, a favorite snack, out of their mound.

59
00:04:40.336 --> 00:04:46.560
Until that moment, scientists believed that one of the things that defined humans and distinguished them from all

60
00:04:46.700 --> 00:04:50.422
other animal species was their ability to make and use tools.

61
00:04:51.322 --> 00:04:56.165
But the chimps' use of tools revealed that humans were not the only intelligent creatures on earth.

62
00:04:56.986 --> 00:05:00.628
And if chimps could use tools, what other human qualities did they share?

63
00:05:01.808 --> 00:05:07.851
Goodall's love of animals had carried her deep into Africa and allowed her to make many discoveries, but

64
00:05:07.911 --> 00:05:09.492
she was not a trained scientist.

65
00:05:10.492 --> 00:05:14.754
Leakey told her that if she wanted to continue her research, she would have to go back to

66
00:05:14.794 --> 00:05:20.917
school. In 1962, Goodall returned to England and entered Cambridge University.

67
00:05:21.858 --> 00:05:26.040
There, she faced criticism from scientists who challenged her research methods.

68
00:05:27.012 --> 00:05:33.395
They argued that Goodall's practice of identifying the chimps by names instead of numbers showed affection for them,

69
00:05:33.935 --> 00:05:37.897
and her feelings could make it impossible for her to be objective about their behavior.

70
00:05:38.837 --> 00:05:45.000
Others said that the emotions Goodall claimed to see in the chimps—such as joy, sadness, or jealousy—could never

71
00:05:45.040 --> 00:05:45.761
be verified.

72
00:05:46.721 --> 00:05:49.402
Undaunted, Goodall completed her Ph.D.

73
00:05:49.702 --> 00:05:53.204
and returned to Africa to study the chimpanzees for another decade.

74
00:05:54.460 --> 00:05:56.921
In 1967, she married and had a son.

75
00:05:57.521 --> 00:06:02.442
Although she still spent time with the chimps, Goodall hired students to carry on the day-to-day work at

76
00:06:02.482 --> 00:06:09.784
Gombe. She began to write about the chimpanzees, completing her first book, In the Shadow of Man, in

77
00:06:09.844 --> 00:06:20.287
1971. In 1986, Harvard University published Goodall's The Chimpanzees of Gombe: Patterns of Behavior to wide acclaim, and

78
00:06:20.347 --> 00:06:23.768
she was recognized as one of the world's foremost primate experts.

79
00:06:24.672 --> 00:06:30.734
By 1980, Goodall had begun to travel around the world, speaking about the chimpanzees of Gombe and their

80
00:06:30.774 --> 00:06:38.496
habitat. People were finally starting to understand humankind's impact on the environment; many animal species were on the

81
00:06:38.576 --> 00:06:43.838
verge of extinction, and natural resources were being spent faster than they could be renewed.

82
00:06:45.018 --> 00:06:50.740
Goodall realized that humans were the only animals capable of destroying the very planet that sustained them.

83
00:06:51.760 --> 00:06:57.286
She believed that people everywhere would have to work together to protect the planet for all living things.

84
00:06:58.247 --> 00:07:04.593
Her tireless work on behalf of animals, humans, and the environment prompted the Secretary General of the United

85
00:07:04.633 --> 00:07:07.996
Nations to name her a Messenger of Peace in 2002.

86
00:07:09.097 --> 00:07:15.323
Today, Jane Goodall continues to share her ideas about how to preserve and improve the world for all

87
00:07:15.363 --> 00:07:16.044
its inhabitants.

88
00:07:16.928 --> 00:07:25.073
The Jane Goodall Institute for Wildlife Research, Education and Conservation, founded in 1971, strives to protect animals and

89
00:07:25.153 --> 00:07:26.193
nurture the environment.

90
00:07:27.474 --> 00:07:33.878
Goodall's Roots Shoots organization offers a youth program with more than eight thousand groups in nearly one hundred

91
00:07:33.938 --> 00:07:40.922
countries. Through Roots Shoots, young people learn ways to better the world by completing projects that promote care

92
00:07:41.222 --> 00:07:44.624
and concern for animals, the environment, and the human community.

93
00:07:45.436 --> 00:07:50.860
"Everybody must remember that every one of us makes a difference, and we have a choice," said Goodall

94
00:07:50.920 --> 00:07:52.401
in a May 2008 interview.

95
00:07:53.462 --> 00:07:59.266
Fifty years after she met David Greybeard in the African wilderness, Goodall points to four things that give

96
00:07:59.286 --> 00:08:06.792
her hope for the future—the human brain, the human spirit, the determination of young people, and the resilience

97
00:08:06.832 --> 00:08:07.272
of nature.
